.Dd $Mdocdate: August 2 2026 $
.Dt Fugu::Log 3p
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm Fugu::Log
.Nd logging to syslog, standard error, or nowhere
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Bd -literal
use Fugu::Log;

my $log = Fugu::Log->new(
    mode     => 'syslog',
    ident    => 'mydaemon',
    level    => 'info',
    facility => 'daemon',
);

$log->info('listening on port %d', $port);
$log->error('cannot open %s: %s', $path, $!);

Fugu::Log->set_default($log);
.Ed
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
gives a program one logging interface.
The interface is the same when the program runs as a daemon and when
it runs in the foreground.
The caller selects the destination one time, when it creates the
logger.
Every call site then has the same form for all destinations.
.Pp
The logger discards messages below the configured level.
The levels, from lowest to highest, are
.Cm debug ,
.Cm info ,
.Cm notice ,
.Cm warning ,
.Cm error
and
.Cm crit .
Each level is a method of the same name.
.Pp
The module also holds one process default logger.
Library code that gets no logger asks for it with
.Fn default ,
so no library has to die for the lack of one.
.Ss new
.Fn new "%args"
creates a logger.
These are the arguments:
.Bl -tag -width "facilityXX"
.It Fa mode
The destination for messages.
The value is one of:
.Pp
.Bl -tag -width "syslogXX" -compact
.It Cm syslog
Messages go through
.Xr syslog 3 .
The logger calls
.Xr openlog 3
immediately with the
.Cm ndelay
and
.Cm pid
options.
.It Cm stderr
Messages go to standard error, with one line for each message.
Each line starts with a local-time stamp and the level in upper case.
.It Cm quiet
Messages go nowhere.
The logger discards them before it does a check of the level.
.El
.Pp
The default is
.Cm stderr .
.It Fa level
The lowest level to emit.
The default is
.Cm info .
.It Fa ident
The program name that the logger passes to
.Xr openlog 3 .
The default is
.Ql fugu .
The logger uses this argument only when
.Fa mode
is
.Cm syslog .
.It Fa facility
The syslog facility.
The value is a
.Xr Sys::Syslog 3p
constant, or one of the names
.Cm daemon ,
.Cm user
or
.Cm local0
through
.Cm local7 .
The default is
.Dv LOG_DAEMON .
.El
.Ss debug, info, notice, warning, error, crit
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$log->info($fmt, @args);
.Ed
.Pp
Each method logs one message at the level that its name gives.
When
.Fa @args
is not empty, the method formats
.Fa $fmt
through
.Xr sprintf 3 .
When
.Fa @args
is empty, the method uses
.Fa $fmt
as a literal string.
.Pp
There is one method for each level.
The spellings
.Cm warn
and
.Cm err
parse as level
.Em names ,
because a configuration file can hold either one.
They are not methods.
.Ss set_level
.Fn set_level "$level"
changes the lowest level to emit on a logger after its creation.
If the value is not a known level name, the logger uses
.Cm info .
.Ss level
.Fn level
returns the lowest level to emit, by name.
The name is one of the six canonical levels, whatever spelling the
caller used.
.Ss mode
.Fn mode
returns the destination, one of the
.Dv MODE_SYSLOG ,
.Dv MODE_STDERR
and
.Dv MODE_QUIET
constants.
The constants hold the strings
.Ql syslog ,
.Ql stderr
and
.Ql quiet .
.Ss reopen
.Fn reopen
closes and opens the log again with the same settings.
A daemon calls this after it drops privileges: the
.Xr syslog 3
connection belongs to the user that opened it.
In the other modes the method does nothing.
.Pp
This replaces the older pattern of discarding the logger and building
an identical one.
That pattern needed every call site to learn the new object.
.Ss default
.Fn default
returns the process default logger.
The first call creates a
.Cm stderr
logger, so the method never returns
.Dv undef .
.Pp
This is the fallback for library code.
A module takes a
.Fa log
argument and uses
.Fn default
when the caller gives none.
Thus no library dies for the lack of a logger, and no library needs a
global of its own.
.Ss set_default
.Fn set_default "$log"
replaces the process default logger and returns the new default.
A program calls this once at startup.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn new ,
.Fn default
and
.Fn set_default
return a logger object.
.Fn reopen
returns the object.
.Fn level
and
.Fn mode
return a name.
The logging methods and
.Fn set_level
have no useful return value.
.Sh EXAMPLES
This example logs to standard error in the foreground, and to syslog
in the background, with no change to the call sites:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
my $log = Fugu::Log->new(
    mode  => $foreground ? 'stderr' : 'syslog',
    ident => 'mydaemon',
    level => $verbose ? 'debug' : 'info',
);
Fugu::Log->set_default($log);
.Ed
.Pp
This example opens the syslog connection again as the unprivileged
user:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
Fugu::Privdrop->drop_privileges(user => '_myapp');
$log->reopen;
.Ed
.Sh ERRORS
.Fn new
dies if
.Fa mode
is not one of the three names above.
An unknown
.Fa level
or
.Fa facility
is not an error.
The level becomes
.Cm info ,
and the facility becomes
.Dv LOG_DAEMON .
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr syslog 3 ,
.Xr Fugu::Daemon 3p ,
.Xr Sys::Syslog 3p ,
.Xr syslog.conf 5
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Dick Olsson Aq Mt hi@senzilla.io
.Sh CAVEATS
The logging methods pass the format string to
.Xr sprintf 3
only when arguments follow it.
Thus a message that has a percent sign is safe alone, but not when an
argument follows.
Log variable text with
.Ql %s .
Do not interpolate the text into the format string.
.Pp
A process must create no more than one syslog-mode logger.
.Xr openlog 3
and
.Xr closelog 3
act on process-wide state.
Thus, when a second logger goes out of scope, it closes the connection
that the first logger still uses.
Use
.Fn reopen
on the one logger instead of building a second one.
